usage: dpl <command> [arguments]

commands:
  build [--release] [--target <target>] [-o <path>] <file.dpm>
                   build a native program (C compiler: $CC or cc; for other
                   targets $DPL_CROSS_CC or clang)
  check [--format=json] <file.dpm>
                   check a program and the modules it imports
  explain <code>   explain a diagnostic code, e.g. E0101
  fmt [--check] <file.dpm>...
                   format files in place; --check only lists files that would change
  help             show this help
  test [--target <target>] [--timeout <seconds>] <file.dpm>
                   run the @test functions of a program, each in its own process
                   (other targets run under $DPL_RUNNER or the target's runner);
                   a test running longer than --timeout (default 60, 0 = none) fails
  targets: linux-x86_64 (host), windows-x86_64, android-arm64
  --version        print the compiler version
  worker ...       serve one request of the online service (see docs/service)

debugging commands (output format is not stable):
  debug lex <file.dpm>     print the tokens of a file
  debug parse <file.dpm>   print the syntax tree of a file
  debug print <file.dpm>   print a file in canonical form
  debug summary <file.dpm> print the interface summaries of a program
  debug types <file.dpm>   print the syntax tree of the main module with types
  debug ir <file.dpm>      print the Core IR of a program
  debug run <file.dpm> [args...]
                           run a program in the reference interpreter
  debug c <file.dpm>       print the C code of a program (C backend)
